Below is a shortlist of the RTL‐side debug modules that implement (or glue together) the abstract‐command register logic, issue the busy flag, and drive cmderr on illegal accesses.  A write to data0 during an in-flight abstract command never gets caught simply because none of these blocks check “if (busy) cmderr=BUSY” before they allow a write.  They will all need to be touched:

```text
hw/prim/hdl/dbg_reg.sv
hw/prim/hdl/dbg_cmd_fsm.sv
hw/prim/hdl/riscv_dbg.sv
hw/prim/hdl/adapter_dmi_apb.sv
```

Explanation:

1) hw/prim/hdl/dbg_reg.sv  
   This is the register file for the debug module.  It implements the dataN, command, and cmderr CSR accesses but does not currently gate writes against the busy signal.

2) hw/prim/hdl/dbg_cmd_fsm.sv  
   This FSM tracks the abstract‐command execution and asserts the “busy” flag.  Even though it drives the busy bit in dmcontrol, it must also export “busy” for dbg_reg.sv to sample.

3) hw/prim/hdl/riscv_dbg.sv  
   Top‐level debug‐module wrapper.  It instantiates both dbg_cmd_fsm and dbg_reg (and the DMI/APB adapter).  You’ll need to wire the busy flag out of the FSM back into the register file here.

4) hw/prim/hdl/adapter_dmi_apb.sv  
   This block controls APB/DMI accesses into the debug CSRs.  It’s responsible for steering read/write transactions into dbg_reg.sv’s ports – you need to make sure it reports a cmderr==BUSY when dbg_reg.sv rejects a write to data0.
